const dashQuizCfg = {
  questions: [
    { q: 'Anscombe\'s quartet is famous because its four datasets…', choices: ['are all straight lines', 'share nearly identical summary statistics while looking completely different', 'cannot be plotted', 'prove pie charts work'], answer: 1, why: 'Same means, variances, correlation (0.816) and regression line — yet one is linear, one curved, one an outlier-driven line, one a vertical column. Summary numbers and pictures are complements, not substitutes.' },
    { q: 'Bars for comparing quantities must start at zero because…', choices: ['it looks tidier', 'length is the encoding — truncate the axis and you truncate the encoded value', 'all software defaults to it', 'zero is always the minimum possible value'], answer: 1, why: 'When a bar\'s length carries the number, cutting the baseline is cutting the value: a 10% lift drawn from a 90% baseline looks like a 100% jump. Line charts may use non-zero baselines because position, not length, encodes the value.' },
    { q: 'A metric tile that reads "1,247" is weak because…', choices: ['it is not rounded', 'it lacks a comparison — target, trend or segment — so it cannot support a judgement', 'it should be a pie chart', 'numbers scare executives'], answer: 1, why: 'Judgement needs a reference point: "1,247 vs 1,100 target, down 3% week-on-week" is actionable; a bare number is trivia wearing a dashboard costume.' },
    { q: 'The pyramid principle says a data presentation should…', choices: ['save the conclusion for the end to build suspense', 'state the conclusion first, then layer the supporting evidence', 'begin with methodology', 'show all the data equally'], answer: 1, why: 'Minto\'s rule inverts the mystery-novel structure: answer first, then the three reasons, then the detail. Decision-makers need the destination before they can evaluate the route.' },
  ],
};
---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/data-science/advanced/dashboards-and-data-storytelling">
  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is an advanced Data Science lesson. It stands on the foundational <em>From Data to
    Decisions</em> and on the intermediate rungs where you learned to look before modelling:
    <em>Exploratory Data Analysis</em>, <em>Charts That Tell the Truth</em>, <em>Cleaning
    Messy Data</em> and <em>SQL for Analysts</em>. Those lessons made your analysis correct;
    this one makes it land — the last mile between a right number and a decision made by a
    distracted human with five seconds to spare.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>explain what Anscombe's quartet proves about summary statistics, and why plotting comes first and last;</li>
    <li>choose a chart by matching the Cleveland–McGill encoding hierarchy to the question being asked;</li>
    <li>spot the standard ways charts lie — truncated bar baselines, dual axes, cherry-picked windows, cumulative curves;</li>
    <li>design a dashboard as a monitoring instrument, with context attached to every metric tile;</li>
    <li>structure a data story with the pyramid principle and the SCQA frame, uncertainty told in plain words.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Analysis that never changes a decision is a hobby. Dashboards and data storytelling are
    where analysis earns its keep — the last mile between a correct number and a human doing
    something differently at 9 a.m. The craft has an unusual property: it is judged in seconds
    and remembered in stories. A viewer glances at your chart for five seconds, distracted, on
    a phone; whatever encoding they absorb in that window <em>is</em> your analysis. So the
    syllabus's visualisation topic is really three skills braided together: <strong>seeing</strong>
    (what makes a visual encoding truthful and legible), <strong>monitoring</strong> (the
    dashboard as a decision instrument), and <strong>persuading</strong> (the story structure
    that moves a room). Get them wrong and you produce the two standard failure modes of the
    industry: dashboards nobody opens, and slide decks that are technically accurate and
    completely uncompelling.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the four rules of dashboards people actually use" widget="equationStep" config={dashCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the four rules are ordered by what it costs to skip
    them. Start from the decision, or the screen is decoration; break the five-second rule,
    or the takeaway never arrives; drop the context, and a bare "1,247" cannot be judged;
    ignore the 3 a.m. reader, and the instrument goes unused. Notice that three of the four
    rules are about the viewer's situation, not about the data.
  </p>

  <h2>Why pictures: the limits of summary numbers</h2>
  <p>
    The classic demonstration is <strong>Anscombe's quartet</strong> (Francis Anscombe, 1973):
    four small datasets constructed to share almost every summary statistic — the same mean of
    x (9.0) and of y (7.5), the same variances, the same correlation (0.816), even the same
    fitted regression line, y = 3 + 0.5x. Tabulated, they are indistinguishable. Plotted, they
    are four different worlds: a clean linear trend; a smooth curve; a perfect line spoiled by
    one outlier; and a vertical column pinned at one x-value. The lesson is not "statistics is
    wrong" but that <strong>summaries compress, and compression hides structure</strong> —
    outliers, curvature, clustering, data-entry errors. The modern descendant, the "Datasaurus
    Dozen" (Matejka &amp; Fitzmaurice, 2017), keeps the summary statistics fixed while
    morphing between shapes as different as a dinosaur and a star, proving the point with
    rendering-quality visuals. The working rule that follows: <em>always plot the data before
    summarising it</em>, and after summarising it too.
  </p>
  <p>
    Visual encoding works because human perception is a parallel processor with known
    strengths. Cleveland and McGill's classic experiments (1984) ranked encodings by how
    accurately readers decode them, and the ranking still governs chart choice:
    <strong>position on a common scale</strong> (grouped bars, dot plots) is best; then
    length; then angle and slope; then area; then colour volume and saturation — the worst.
    This is the entire case against pie charts in one sentence: a pie asks the viewer to
    compare angles and areas, the two least accurate judgements people can make, while a bar
    chart asks for position and length, the two most accurate. It is also the case for scatter
    plots and line charts, which put data onto aligned positions.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> a chart misleads only when its author intends to
    deceive. Most chart-lies in practice are defaults: the software truncates the axis
    to "zoom in", picks a red-green palette, starts the trend at the most flattering
    month — and the author ships the result unchecked. Honesty in visualisation is
    mostly checklist behaviour — baseline stated, bars from zero, windows labelled,
    comparisons annotated — because the defaults are not neutral and no amount of
    good intent catches them by feel.
  </p>

  <h2>Choosing the chart: from question to encoding</h2>
  <p>
    Chart selection is not aesthetic taste; it is matching an encoding to a question. Four
    question families cover most of practice:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Comparison</strong> ("which region is biggest?"): bar charts, sorted, with the
    category labels on the bars — horizontal when labels are long. Never a pie for more than
    three slices.</li>
    <li><strong>Trend over time</strong> ("is it growing?"): line charts — position encodes
    the value, and non-zero baselines are acceptable because no length is being compared.</li>
    <li><strong>Relationship</strong> ("do these move together?"): scatter plot, with a trend
    line only if you have earned one (the statistics ladder's regression lessons).</li>
    <li><strong>Distribution</strong> ("what is typical, what is the spread?"): histogram or
    density plot — and note that means and medians hide exactly the structure a distribution
    shows.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> more chart types and decoration mean a more professional
    report. The evidence runs the other way. 3-D bars, dual axes, rainbow palettes and "chart
    junk" all add decoding work while carrying zero information — and every added decoding
    step is a chance for the five-second read to land on the wrong conclusion. Tufte's
    "data–ink ratio" principle and Few's dashboard design rules both say the same thing:
    <strong>erase everything that is not the data or the message</strong>. Boring charts are
    trusted charts.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> summaries compress, and compression hides structure — so plot the data first, and plot it again after summarising. Chart choice itself is matching an encoding to a question, ranked by how accurately humans decode each one. What remains is the ethics of the defaults.</p>

  <h2>Honest axes: how charts lie</h2>
  <p>
    Every encoding choice is an ethical choice, because encodings imply magnitudes. The
    canonical sin: <strong>truncated bar-chart axes</strong>. Draw bars from a baseline of 90
    instead of 0 and a 10% improvement renders as a bar ten times taller than its competitor —
    the length encoding has been quietly rescaled to lie. (Line charts are different: they
    encode position, not length, so a non-zero baseline is legitimate — which is exactly why
    the dishonest version of this trick swaps line charts for bar charts.) Related sins, each
    with a name: <strong>dual axes</strong> letting an author imply correlation between two
    arbitrarily scaled series; <strong>area proportionality</strong> violations (doubling a
    country's GDP and doubling its circle's <em>radius</em> quadruples the area); <strong>cherry-picked
    windows</strong> ("growth since March" starting from the trough); and <strong>cumulative
    charts</strong> that make a decaying rate look like an accelerating one. The defence is
    symmetrical: state the baseline, start bars at zero, label axes with units and time
    ranges, and annotate the comparison you intend ("vs same week last year") so the reader
    inherits your frame explicitly rather than imagining one.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> honesty in charts is mostly checklist behaviour — baselines stated, bars from zero, windows labelled, comparisons annotated — because the software's defaults are not neutral and intent catches nothing on its own. The same discipline now scales up from one chart to a screen of them.</p>

  <h2>Dashboards: instruments, not galleries</h2>
  <p>
    A dashboard is a <em>monitoring instrument</em> — closer to a car's speedometer than to a
    poster. The design rules in the widget compress the discipline: start from the decision
    the screen supports; headline the takeaway; attach context to every number; and optimise
    for the distracted reader. Context deserves emphasis because it is the most-skipped rule.
    A bare "1,247" cannot be judged; "1,247 vs 1,100 target, down 3% week-on-week, down in 4
    of 6 regions" supports a judgement in one read. Comparisons are what turn data into
    information, and a good dashboard builds them in structurally: against target, against
    last period, against a comparable segment.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two operational truths round it out. First, <strong>dashboards rot</strong>: metrics get
    renamed, pipelines break silently, and a dashboard showing stale data is worse than none
    (it manufactures false confidence). Ownership, freshness indicators and alerting on data
    staleness are part of dashboard design, not IT housekeeping. Second, <strong>dashboard
    sprawl is a design failure</strong>: when an organisation has 200 dashboards, it has an
    unowned metrics layer. The mature fix is a small set of canonical, defined metrics with
    one home each — and the discipline to retire screens when the decision they supported
    changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    <em>Common misconception:</em> real-time is always better — a dashboard updating
    every second is more professional than one updating daily. Freshness should match
    the decision, not the technology's capability: a weekly planning meeting is served
    by weekly data, while a seconds-fresh pipeline costs real engineering and invites
    noise into every glance (a metric wobbling minute to minute is noise wearing
    urgency). The right question is "how stale may this number be before a wrong
    decision gets made?" — then build to that answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    A small worked example of context done right: the tile reads "Checkout conversion:
    2.84%, target 3.00%, last week 2.91%, −0.07 week-on-week". In one read the viewer
    has performed the three judgements that matter — below target, falling, and by how
    much relative to normal week-to-week noise — and can route attention (is this
    ordinary wiggle, or the new payment flow?). The same tile written as "2.84"
    delivers none of that, and its reader must either know the history or invent it.
  </p>

  <p><strong>So far:</strong> a dashboard is a monitoring instrument — decision first, takeaway headlined, context attached to every number, staleness owned and alerted on. Instruments watch; the next section argues, and arguing needs a different structure.</p>

  <h2>Storytelling: the structure that moves a room</h2>
  <p>
    Analysis presented as a chronology — "first I looked at the data, then I tried a model,
    then…" — buries the payoff. Two structures fix it. The <strong>pyramid principle</strong>
    (Minto): state the conclusion first, then the two-to-four supporting arguments, then the
    detail — the answer before the reasoning, at every level of the document. The
    <strong>SCQA frame</strong> gives the narrative spine: Situation ("signups grew 20% all
    year"), Complication ("but the growth is concentrated in one segment, and it is
    slowing"), Question ("where do we invest next quarter?"), Answer ("here, with these three
    moves, at roughly this cost"). The frame converts charts into an argument; the pyramid
    converts an argument into a decision. Both invert the mystery-novel instinct that feels
    natural to analysts — and both exist because executives evaluate recommendations, not
    journeys.
  </p>
  <p>
    Uncertainty belongs in the story, told in plain words. "Early signal: lift of 1.1 points,
    but the interval still includes zero — we will have enough data in three weeks" is a
    sentence that builds trust; false precision ("up 1.14%!") spends it. The compounding
    asset of an analyst's career is being the person whose caveats turned out to matter.
  </p>
  <p>
    The founding case study is <strong>John Snow's cholera map</strong> (1854). A deadly
    outbreak was raging in Soho, London, and the received explanations — bad air,
    "miasma" — came with tables and debates. Snow, who suspected the Broad Street water
    pump, drew the deaths as bars on the street map, and the picture did what no table
    could: the fatal addresses clustered visibly around one pump, while workers at a
    nearby brewery, who drank their own supply instead of the water, stayed
    conspicuously spared. The visual argument helped persuade the local board to remove
    the pump handle, and the method — plot the evidence where it lives, let the pattern
    argue — is the ancestor of every spatial dashboard since. Note what made it work:
    not decoration, but the right encoding and the right comparison, the same two
    choices this chapter keeps returning to.
  </p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>Two datasets share the same mean, variance, correlation and regression line. What does Anscombe's quartet prove you must still do before summarising them for a decision-maker?</li>
    <li>A report shows a bar chart of revenue with the axis starting at 90 rather than 0, claiming a dramatic rise. Say precisely what is dishonest, and the two fixes that restore honesty.</li>
    <li>Why does the pyramid principle invert the chronological write-up of an analysis, and what does the SCQA frame add that the pyramid alone lacks?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Plot it — summaries compress, and compression hides
    outliers, curvature, clusters and data-entry errors; identical statistics can describe
    four different worlds, so the picture is part of the evidence, not decoration. (2) A
    bar's length is the encoding, so truncating the baseline rescales every bar and renders
    a 10-point difference as a ten-fold height gap; the fixes are starting bars at zero and
    annotating the intended comparison ("vs same week last year") so the reader inherits
    your frame explicitly. (3) Because executives evaluate recommendations, not journeys —
    the answer comes first, then the two-to-four supporting arguments, then the detail.
    SCQA adds the narrative spine that earns the answer: situation, complication, question,
    answer — so the room accepts the destination because it has seen the stakes of the
    route.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Always plot the data before and after summarising — identical statistics can hide different worlds.</li>
    <li>Match encoding to question: position beats length beats angle beats area beats colour.</li>
    <li>Bars start at zero; line charts may float — length encodes value, position does not.</li>
    <li>A metric without a comparison is trivia; context — target, trend, segment — turns it into information.</li>
    <li>Dashboards rot and sprawl; ownership, freshness indicators and retirement are part of the design.</li>
    <li>Answer first: the pyramid principle for structure, SCQA for the spine, plain-word uncertainty throughout.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling advanced lessons complete the working analyst's kit: <em>A/B Testing</em>
    shows how a lift worth presenting is generated honestly in the first place, <em>Statistics
    for Data Science</em> supplies the intervals and tests behind every number on your tiles,
    and <em>Machine Learning Basics</em> introduces the models whose behaviour future
    dashboards will have to monitor.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Perception is measurable.</strong> Cleveland–McGill is just the start: preattentive
    processing research shows which visual properties "pop" in under 250 milliseconds (colour,
    size, orientation, motion) — the reason a single red bar in a grey series directs attention
    without a single word. Gestalt principles (proximity, similarity, enclosure) explain why
    some layouts read as groups instantly. Modern eye-tracking work on dashboards confirms the
    five-second window empirically: F-patterns of gaze, headline dominance, and the cost of
    any encoding requiring a legend lookup. Visual design for data is applied perceptual
    psychology, and its findings replicate.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Uncertainty visualisation is the frontier.</strong> Showing intervals honestly
    (error bars, fan charts, animation of plausible outcomes) reliably causes misreading —
    people read 95% intervals as "the data varies this much", or ignore the bars entirely.
    Research directions include hypothetical outcome plots (animated draws from the
    distribution), quantile dotplots, and gradient-encoded bands; results are promising and
    unresolved. The related ethical frontier is automated chart generation: when a BI tool
    picks the axis range, the comparison window and the highlight colour by default, its
    defaults are quietly making thousands of micro-editorial decisions a day.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Open questions.</strong> How should narrative and interactivity be combined
    without losing the static first read? Can uncertainty communication be standardised the
    way financial reporting was? And as LLMs draft both the analysis and the chart, what does
    an audit trail for visual claims look like — who is responsible when a generated headline
    overstates an interval? The craft in this chapter is old (Playfair invented the line and
    bar chart in the 1780s; Minard's 1869 Napoleon map is still the pinnacle); its automation
    is brand new.
  </p>

  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Minto, <em>The Pyramid Principle</em>; Knaflic, <em>Storytelling with Data</em>; Few, <em>Information Dashboard Design</em>; Tufte, <em>The Visual Display of Quantitative Information</em>; Anscombe, "Graphs in Statistical Analysis" (1973); Cleveland &amp; McGill, "Graphical Perception" (1984); Matejka &amp; Fitzmaurice, "Same Stats, Different Graphs" (2017). Source text: OpenStax (https://openstax.org), CC BY 4.0 — adapted for Interactive Atlas.</p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="check yourself: encodings, honesty and structure" widget="quiz" config={dashQuizCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> each question is a trap the chapter dismantled —
    summaries that look complete, truncated bar baselines, bare numbers, suspense-first
    storytelling. The wrong choices are all defaults or instincts; the right ones are the
    checklist habits that make a five-second read land on the truth.
  </p>
</TopicPage>
